{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"famous-enslaved-unau","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Single-source the ticket-type map and extend the one-literal guard to cover it\n\n## Context\n\nThis is the SAME defect class [rebar:fe15-3bc4-ed70-4b61] just closed, still live in a third map —\nand the guard that was supposed to prevent recurrence does not cover it.\n\n`test_jira_family_boundary_heldout.py` enforces \"exactly one dict literal defines each value map\nwithin `adapters/`\" for the STATUS map (`:300`) and the PRIORITY map (`:308`). There is no such\nassertion for the TICKET-TYPE map, and it is duplicated:\n\n* `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py:88` — `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE: dict[str, str] = {...}`, read at\n  `:129` for the Cloud create path.\n* `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:43` — `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE = {\"task\": \"Task\", \"story\": ...}`,\n  read at `:61` for the DC create path.\n\nBoth were verified present in the tree. `config.py:11` also references a `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE` in a\ncomment, which is worth checking for a third copy while doing this.\n\n## Why it matters more than it looks\n\nThe two copies are read by the two CREATE paths. A divergence means the same local ticket type\nbecomes a different Jira issue type depending on deployment — silently, at creation, with no\ncomparison anywhere that would notice. fe15's status map had already drifted by one key\n(`\"deleted\"`) before anyone looked; there is no reason to assume this one is luckier, and nothing\nwould tell us.\n\n## Approach\n\nFollow fe15's resolution exactly, because it is the established local idiom and it was reviewed:\nrelocate the single definition into `adapters/jira_family/value_maps.py` alongside\n`LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` and `LOCAL_PRIORITY_TO_JIRA`, have both create paths import it, then extend\nthe boundary guard so a future third copy fails the build.\n\n**Compare the two copies BEFORE unifying them.** If they have already drifted, the unification is a\nBEHAVIOUR change for one deployment and needs the divergence decided explicitly and recorded — the\nway J2 decided `\"deleted\" -> \"Done\"` — not silently resolved by whichever copy is moved.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `adapters/jira_family/value_maps.py` — the single definition.\n- both create paths — import it.\n- `test_jira_family_boundary_heldout.py` — extend the one-literal guard to the type map.\n- a characterization pin for the type mapping on BOTH create paths (overlaps [rebar:cedc-58d1-f6d1-428e];\n  whichever lands first carries it).\n\n## Testing\n\n* the guard must FAIL when a second literal is reintroduced — mutation-checked, not asserted.\n* both create paths keep producing their current issue-type values, pinned by value.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the two existing copies are DIFFED and the result recorded here; if they diverge, the chosen\n      value is decided explicitly with its rationale, not defaulted to\n- [ ] exactly one `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE`-equivalent literal exists under `adapters/`, in\n      `jira_family/value_maps.py`, and both create paths read it\n- [ ] the boundary guard covers the ticket-type map and is MUTATION-CHECKED: reintroducing a second\n      literal turns it red, verdict recorded\n- [ ] the type mapping is pinned by value for both Cloud and DC create paths\n- [ ] `config.py`'s referenced type map is checked for a third copy, and the finding recorded either\n      way","id":"bd9e-0740-1b47-47f8","parent_id":"3e73-72b5-cff2-40f0","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Single-source the ticket-type map; guard it like the others"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785642296891809001,"uuid":"7979e7da-78aa-4c7b-bddd-ba1583864680"}